.\" OpenBSD-style concise man page
.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt REALPATH 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm realpath
.Nd resolve canonical file names
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm realpath
.Op Fl emsqz
.Op Fl Fl relative-to Ns = Ns Ar dir
.Op Fl Fl relative-base Ns = Ns Ar dir
.Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility prints the resolved absolute pathname for each
.Ar file
argument.
All
.Sq \&.
and
.Sq ..
components are resolved, along with extra
.Sq /
characters and symbolic links.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl e , Fl Fl canonicalize-existing
All components of the path must exist.
This is the default behavior.
.It Fl m , Fl Fl canonicalize-missing
No component of the path needs to exist.
Missing components are resolved logically.
.It Fl s , Fl Fl no-symlinks , Fl Fl strip
Do not resolve symbolic links.
Only resolve
.Sq \&.
and
.Sq ..
components and produce an absolute path.
.It Fl z , Fl Fl zero
End each output line with a NUL character instead of a newline.
.It Fl q , Fl Fl quiet
Suppress most error messages.
.It Fl Fl relative-to Ns = Ns Ar dir
Print the resolved path relative to
.Ar dir .
.It Fl Fl relative-base Ns = Ns Ar dir
Print the resolved path relative to
.Ar dir
if the path is a descendant of
.Ar dir ;
otherwise print the absolute path.
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
Display a help message and exit.
.It Fl V , Fl Fl version
Output version information and exit.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std realpath
.Sh EXAMPLES
Resolve a path with
.Sq ..
components:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ realpath /usr/bin/../lib
/usr/lib
.Ed
.Pp
Resolve a path that does not exist:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ realpath -m /tmp/new/path
/tmp/new/path
.Ed
.Pp
Resolve without following symlinks:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ realpath -s ./relative/../path
/current/dir/path
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr basename 1 ,
.Xr dirname 1 ,
.Xr readlink 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is compliant with the
.St -p1003.1-2008
specification.
The
.Fl m ,
.Fl s ,
.Fl q ,
.Fl z ,
.Fl Fl relative-to ,
and
.Fl Fl relative-base
options are extensions compatible with GNU coreutils.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An "vibeutils implementation by Travis Cole"
